ITEM 2  instance_bound() - what it really asserts, and how much room it has
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
The docstring's derivation ("2E = sum over orbits of |orbit| * valence, |orbit| <= |point group|")
is the right ANSWER by a different route than the one written. The exact statement:

  * The frames the fill reaches at a fixed quotient dart form a GROUP Gamma0 <= SO(3). Closure:
    a word w that returns the walk to dart 0 with frame g, followed by a word u that does the same
    with frame g', returns to dart 0 with frame g*g'. The walk is reversible (glue and M are
    involutions; rneig has finite period), so every dart carries a coset of Gamma0 and they all
    have the same size. Hence   ninst = Q * |Gamma0|,   Q = len(rneig) = the quotient dart count.
  * MEASURED: the alphabet stores each vertex half-edge TWICE, so Q = sum over orbits of
    2*valence/fold  <=  2*total. (Verified over 4,000 star-wide vertex types: word length 3 gives
    quotient darts 6/3/2/1 at folds 1/2/3/6, and so on.)
  * instance_bound returns max(120, 4*maxrot) * total = 2*total * max(60, 2*maxrot). So the bound
    is EXACTLY the claim  |Gamma0| <= max(60, 2*maxrot),  and Gamma0 is a finite subgroup of SO(3),
    i.e. C_n (order n), D_n (order 2n), T (12), O (24) or I (60).
      - POLYHEDRAL Gamma0: |Gamma0| <= 60 <= max(60, 2*maxrot). SOUND unconditionally; maxrot is
        not used at all.
      - AXIAL Gamma0 = C_n or D_n: need 2n <= max(60, 2*maxrot), i.e. n <= 30 OR n <= maxrot.
  * So the only way the bound can truncate a closing fill is an n-fold axis with n > 30 AND
    n > maxrot (on star-wide maxrot <= 10, so: n > 30). The docstring's reason why that cannot
    happen ("the axis passes through a vertex, an edge midpoint or a face centre") is NOT airtight
    for a STAR polyhedron: the faces are a branched cover of density D, so the axis point can be
    covered by n/m faces that C_n permutes with none of them centred there, giving only
    n <= n_face * D. With densities up to 38 on this shelf that is not vacuous. Hence the
    empirical test below.

MEASURED HEADROOM on the 148 realized certificates: median ninst/bound = 0.136, and FOUR k=1
records sit at EXACTLY 1.000 -
    10.6.4      V=120 E=180 F=62  d=1   ninst=360 bound=360   (truncated icosidodecahedron)
    10/3.6.4    V=120 E=180 F=62  d=13  ninst=360 bound=360   (U68)
    10/3.10.4   V=120 E=180 F=54  d=3   ninst=360 bound=360
    10/3.10.6   V=120 E=180 F=44  d=4   ninst=360 bound=360
These are |Gamma0| = 60 = |I|, the largest a rotation group can be, so the bound is TIGHT and
cannot be lowered. It also means the guard comparison must stay `>` and never `>=`; both
eu_sphfill (`if (++pops > guard)`) and develop_sphere (`if pops > guard`) use `>`. Checked.

