---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ITEM 2 (cont.)  instance_bound: sharpening the one gap, then testing it
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Restating the earlier derivation in the form that can actually fail.
  ninst = Q * |Gamma0|, Q = len(rneig) <= 2*total, Gamma0 = the frame group at dart 0, which acts
  FREELY on instances (a dart plus a frame pins the rotation, so only the identity fixes one).
  instance_bound = max(120, 4*maxrot)*total = 2*total * max(60, 2*maxrot), so the whole content of
  the bound is    |Gamma0| <= max(60, 2*maxrot),  Gamma0 in {C_n, D_n, T(12), O(24), I(60)}.
  Polyhedral Gamma0: <= 60. UNCONDITIONALLY SOUND, maxrot unused.
  Axial Gamma0: need n <= 30 or n <= maxrot. star-wide has maxrot <= 10, so the bound can only
  break on an n-fold axis with n >= 31.
Where does such an axis live? Let r have order n >= 31.
  * axis through a VERTEX: r cyclically permutes that vertex's darts, so n | valence <= 6.
    Impossible. (This is the case the docstring names, and for it the docstring is right.)
  * axis through an EDGE midpoint: order <= 2. Impossible.
  * axis through a FACE INTERIOR: this is the case the docstring gets wrong for a star. r permutes
    the D_p face-sheets covering the axis point p; the stabiliser of one sheet is cyclic of order
    dividing that face's n_f, so   n <= n_f * D_p <= 10 * D_p   on this palette.
    n >= 31 therefore needs an axis point covered by at least FOUR sheets, cyclically permuted, on
    a solid whose faces come from {3,4,5,6,8,10,5/2,8/3,10/3}. Not excluded by any argument I can
    make; the shelf carries densities up to 38, so D_p >= 4 is not absurd.
So the bound is a THEOREM for polyhedral Gamma0 and a CONJECTURE for axial Gamma0 with n >= 31.
Empirical test (guard x10) below.
